[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out the design. Since logs will now leave the user's browser, redaction stops being hygiene
  and becomes the thing that decides what lands in your log store forever.

  The fork is fail-closed vs. fail-open. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m already logs on a code path that holds a credential,
  so this isn't hypothetical.

  - [1mAllowlist at the remote boundary[0m — console keeps everything (it's the user's own machine and already visible to
    them), but only explicitly-declared fields serialize into anything shipped remotely. Fails closed: a field nobody
    thought about is dropped, not transmitted. Costs friction — every new remote-visible field is a deliberate addition.
  - [1mDenylist scrubbing[0m — recursively redact keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, and friends. Low friction,
    catches the obvious accidents. Fails open: [38;5;153mpwd[39m, [38;5;153mcredentials.pass[39m, or a password interpolated into a message string
    sails straight through.
  - [1mDiscipline only[0m — a documented rule plus code review, no mechanism. Zero friction, zero enforcement.

  My recommendation: [1mallowlist at the remote boundary[0m. Redaction is the one part of this design that's genuinely hard to
  undo — once a secret is in a log store you have a disclosure to handle, not a bug to fix — and the asymmetry
  justifies the friction. I'd pair it with denylist scrubbing on the console path too, as a cheap backstop.
